```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

export default class ManageRegistrationScreen extends React.Component {
  state = {
    registrations: [],
    modifiedData: []
  };

  checkMNPStatusAPI(phoneNo, index) {
    console.log("mnpphoneno: " + phoneNo);

    var headers = new Headers();
    headers.append("Authorization", "Bearer " + user.getAccessToken());
    fetch(api.checkPortInStatus(user.getProductCode(), phoneNo), {
      headers: headers,
      method: 'GET'
    })
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(rjson => {
        const modifiedRegistration = { ...this.state.registrations[index] };
        modifiedRegistration.mnpStatus = rjson.status;
        this.setState({ modifiedData: [...this.state.modifiedData, modifiedRegistration] });
      })
  }

  handleUpdateRegistrations() {
    this.setState({ registrations: [...this.state.modifiedData] });
    this.setState({ modifiedData: [] });
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        {this.state.registrations.map((registration, index) => (
          <div key={index}>
            <button onClick={() => this.checkMNPStatusAPI(registration.phoneNo, index)}>
              Check MNP Status
            </button>
            <p>MNP Status: {registration.mnpStatus}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
        <button onClick={this.handleUpdateRegistrations}>Update Registrations</button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
```

In this example, we added a new state variable `modifiedData` to store the modified data. When the `checkMNPStatusAPI` function is called, it creates a new modified registration object and adds it to the `modifiedData` array. The `handleUpdateRegistrations` function updates the `registrations` state with the `modifiedData` array and clears the `modifiedData` array.

In the render function, we map the `registrations` array and display the MNP status for each registration. We also add a button to update the registrations, which calls the `handleUpdateRegistrations` function.

